<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Companion cavalry</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Companion cavalry, or hetairoi, was the elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> of the Macedonian army, recruited from the Macedonian aristocracy. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> it delivered the decisive charges at battles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> maintained a similar elite cavalry unit modelled on the Macedonian tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry.jpg" alt="Companion cavalry" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Companions, in Greek hetairoi, served as the elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> of the Macedonian army from the time of Philip II, and gained their highest prestige under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. They are regarded as the first or among the first shock cavalry used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The name means those near the king.</p><p>Companions could be members of the Macedonian aristocracy or commoners of any origin who enjoyed the trust and friendship of the king, and a festival called the Hetairideia marked the bond between king and companions. Even the Athenian playwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> was honoured as a hetairos of King Archelaus. The king's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a>, or basilikoi hetairoi, were named for life by the king from among the aristocracy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Companions came from the most powerful nobles, who could acquire and maintain the best horses and armour. In Alexander's day they mostly carried a xyston, a long thrusting spear, and wore a bronze muscle cuirass or linothorax, shoulder guards and a Boeotian helmet, but no shield. A kopis or xiphos served for close combat if the xyston broke. In the age of Philip II and Alexander they were organised in eight territorial squadrons, called ilai, each of 200 to 300 horsemen.</p><p>The Royal Ile, which Alexander himself commanded, was about twice as large, at c. 400. Squadrons could be combined in twos, threes or fours into a hipparchy under a hipparch. Philotas first commanded the whole force, and after his execution command was split between Cleitus the Black and Hephaestion. Arrian says Alexander did not want anyone, even his intimate friend, to be the centre of attention. As the army campaigned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, non-Macedonians played a growing part, and the Macedonian mutiny at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> may have been partly caused by this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In battle the Companions formed the hammer to the Macedonian phalanx's anvil: the phalanx pinned the enemy while the cavalry attacked a flank or the rear. Alexander personally led the charge at the head of the royal squadron, usually in a wedge, and in a pitched battle the Companions usually fought on the right wing, next to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">hypaspists</a>. Their role was decisive in most of his battles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><p>The Companions of the successor states were more heavily equipped. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE the Seleucid Companions wore lighter equipment than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> but otherwise similar. Ptolemaic and Antigonid Companions carried a large round cavalry shield. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> replaced the title with grades of King's Friends but kept Companions as the name of a regiment, and the Byzantine Hetaireia, a bodyguard of the 9th to 12th centuries composed chiefly of foreigners, echoed the name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Companion cavalry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Macedon's economy was predominantly agrarian, with fertile plains yielding cereals and wine, while extensive pasture bred the horses essential to the <b>Companion cavalry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Seleucus I Nicator, who had commanded the royal hypaspists under Alexander and the <b>Companion cavalry</b> under the regent Perdiccas, was appointed satrap of Babylonia in 320 BCE but was driven out by the dominant Diadochos Antigonus I Monophthalmus in 316 BCE, and spent several years in exile at…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
